{%- comment -%}
  fye-buybox-eternity — choices for rings whose variants are
  Metal x Diamond Quality: eternity, diamond and gem-set wedding rings.

  Rendered by main-product when the product is NOT a plain band.

  ── THE DATA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

      Metal            Platinum, 18k Gold, 14k Gold, 9k Gold, Palladium
      Diamond Quality  D/E VVS, F/G VS, G Si, H Si, Lab-grown D VVS

  Both are variant options — 25 variants on a typical eternity ring — and both
  change the price. Gold COLOUR does not, so it is a line-item property.

  ── EVERYTHING IS A BUTTON. NOTHING NAVIGATES. ────────────────────────────

  Copied from live's product-block, which does this correctly:

      row 1   Yellow · White · Rose · Platinum · Palladium
      row 2   18K · 14K · 9K          (shown only on a gold)

    · a gold colour sets the "Gold colour" PROPERTY. It does not touch the
      variant — unless you were on platinum, in which case the metal moves to
      the preferred carat, exactly as live does (W785).
    · platinum and palladium set the METAL, and the carat row hides itself.
    · carat tiles set the metal within the golds, keeping colour and quality.

  An earlier pass made these links carrying ?variant= and ?gold=, which worked
  but reloaded the page on every colour change. Ed: live does it without that.
  It does, because the state lives in JS and the variant is resolved from
  metal x quality on the fly. So: buttons, and fye-ui.js keeps the hidden
  inputs in step.

  Both rows render for EVERY metal — the carat row is hidden, not absent, so
  the script can reveal it without a page load.

  ── PROPERTY NAMES ARE A CONTRACT ─────────────────────────────────────────

  Verbatim from live's product-block.js — the workshop reads these off orders:

      "Gold colour"      "Yellow", not "Yellow Gold"
      "Ring size"
      "Engraving", "Engraving font"
      "_size_surcharge"  "Yes"

  ── ORDER OF THE HIDDEN METAL INPUT ───────────────────────────────────────

  fye-ui.js builds a variant title by joining every [data-fye-option] control
  in DOM order. Shopify's titles are "Metal / Quality", so the hidden metal
  input MUST come before the quality select. It shipped after it once and the
  price silently stopped updating, because "G Si / 18k Gold" matches nothing.

  Parameters: product, settings (the section's), current (chosen variant)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign s = settings
  assign p = product

  assign metal_opt = nil
  assign quality_opt = nil
  for option in p.options_with_values
    if option.name == 'Metal'
      assign metal_opt = option
    elsif option.name contains 'Quality'
      assign quality_opt = option
    endif
  endfor

  assign metal_now = metal_opt.selected_value
  assign quality_now = quality_opt.selected_value

  assign is_gold = false
  if metal_now contains 'Gold'
    assign is_gold = true
  endif

  assign golds = s.gold_colours | default: 'Yellow,White,Rose' | split: ','
  assign sizes = s.ring_sizes | default: 'A,A.5,B,B.5,C,C.5,D,D.5,E,E.5,F,F.5,G,G.5,H,H.5,I,I.5,J,J.5,K,K.5,L,L.5,M,M.5,N,N.5,O,O.5,P,P.5,Q,Q.5,R,R.5,S,S.5,T,T.5,U,U.5,V,V.5,W,W.5,X,X.5,Y,Y.5,Z,Z.5,Z+1,Z+1.5,Z+2,Z+2.5,Z+3,Z+3.5,Z+4,Z+4.5,Z+5,Z+5.5,Z+6,Z+6.5,Z+7,Z+7.5,Z+8,Z+8.5,Z+9,Z+9.5' | split: ','
  assign threshold = s.surcharge_size | default: 'Q'

  comment
    The carat to land on when someone picks a gold colour from platinum.
    Live calls this preferredKarat() and lands on 18ct; here it is simply the
    first gold the product offers, which is the same thing without hardcoding.
  endcomment
  assign preferred_karat = ''
  for value in metal_opt.values
    if value contains 'Gold'
      assign preferred_karat = value
      break
    endif
  endfor
-%}

{%- if metal_opt != nil -%}
  <div class="pdp__field">
    <p class="pdp__label pdp__label--inline">Metal: <span class="pdp__labelval" data-fye-metal-label>{{ metal_now }}</span></p>

    <ul class="pdp__colours" data-fye-metals data-preferred-karat="{{ preferred_karat | escape }}">
      {%- for colour in golds -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign name = colour | strip
          assign tone = name | downcase | prepend: '--metal-18-'
        -%}
        <li>
          <button type="button"
                  class="pdp__colour{% if is_gold and forloop.first %} is-current{% endif %}"
                  style="--sw: var({{ tone }});"
                  data-fye-gold="{{ name | escape }}">
            <span class="pdp__dot"></span>
            <span class="pdp__colourname">{{ name }}</span>
          </button>
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}

      {%- for value in metal_opt.values -%}
        {%- unless value contains 'Gold' -%}
          {%- liquid
            if value == 'Platinum'
              assign tone = '--metal-platinum'
            else
              assign tone = '--platinum'
            endif
          -%}
          <li>
            <button type="button"
                    class="pdp__colour{% if value == metal_now %} is-current{% endif %}"
                    style="--sw: var({{ tone }});"
                    data-fye-metal="{{ value | escape }}">
              <span class="pdp__dot"></span>
              <span class="pdp__colourname">{{ value }}</span>
            </button>
          </li>
        {%- endunless -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Rendered for every metal and HIDDEN on the non-golds, so the script can
    reveal it without a page load.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="pdp__field" data-fye-karatrow{% unless is_gold %} hidden{% endunless %}>
    <ul class="pdp__carats">
      {%- for value in metal_opt.values -%}
        {%- if value contains 'Gold' -%}
          <li>
            <button type="button"
                    class="pdp__carat{% if value == metal_now %} is-current{% endif %}"
                    data-fye-metal="{{ value | escape }}">{{ value | remove: ' Gold' | upcase }}</button>
          </li>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ul>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Carries the gold colour to the cart. Disabled on a non-gold, and a disabled
    field is not posted — so a platinum order has no colour on it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <input type="hidden"
         name="properties[Gold colour]"
         value="{{ golds[0] | strip | escape }}"
         data-fye-gold-input
         {% unless is_gold %}disabled{% endunless %}>

  {%- comment -%} MUST precede the quality select. See the header. {%- endcomment -%}
  <input type="hidden" value="{{ metal_now | escape }}" data-fye-option data-fye-metal-input>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if quality_opt != nil -%}
  <div class="pdp__field">
    <label class="pdp__label" for="pdp-quality">
      {{ quality_opt.name }}
      {%- if s.quality_help_url != blank -%}
        <a class="pdp__sizelink" href="{{ s.quality_help_url }}">What do these mean?</a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </label>
    <select class="pdp__select" id="pdp-quality" name="options[{{ quality_opt.name | escape }}]" data-fye-option data-fye-quality>
      {%- for value in quality_opt.values -%}
        <option value="{{ value | escape }}"{% if quality_now == value %} selected{% endif %}>{{ value }}</option>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </select>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if sizes.size > 0 -%}
  <div class="pdp__field">
    <label class="pdp__label" for="pdp-size">
      Ring size
      {%- if s.size_guide_url != blank -%}
        <a class="pdp__sizelink" href="{{ s.size_guide_url }}">Find my size</a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </label>
    <select class="pdp__select"
            id="pdp-size"
            name="properties[Ring size]"
            data-fye-size
            data-threshold="{{ threshold }}"
            required>
      <option value="">Select your size</option>
      {%- for size in sizes -%}
        <option>{{ size | strip }}</option>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </select>

    <input type="hidden" name="properties[_size_surcharge]" value="Yes" data-fye-surcharge disabled>

    <p class="pdp__hint fine" data-fye-surcharge-note hidden>
      {{ s.surcharge_note | default: 'Sizes above Q use more metal, so a 10% surcharge applies. It is shown in the cart.' }}
    </p>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
